WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:03.990
GARNETTE CADOGAN:
Many times, what

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.670
is happening, at
the heart of racism,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.850
are people not merely denying
other people's dignity

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.090
and humanity, but denying their
very own dignity and humanity.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.100
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.657
an innovative approach to
teaching about race and racism.

00:00:20.657 --> 00:00:22.740
GARNETTE CADOGAN: It means
pulling back and asking

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.610
some tough questions
about our society,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:28.350
about ourselves, about what
it takes to move forward,

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:29.880
understanding each
other, but also

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.580
working to advance a much
more just society and a much

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.440
better way of understanding
what it means to coexist.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.410
SARAH HANSEN: In this episode,
an urban studies course

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.590
examines our lives
in public spaces

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.520
and invites us to consider how
our very personal experiences

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.245
might also contain the
experiences of others.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:01.360
In our conversation about his
course 11.S947 "The Fire This

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:01.860
Time--

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.700
Race and Racism in American
Cities", visiting scholar

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.400
and acclaimed essayist
Garnette Cadogan

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.280
shares his approach for
helping students wrestle with,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.300
as he puts it, the story of
racism in the United States.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.610
Part of what I love
about Garnette's approach

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.540
is how he weaves his own life
experiences into his teaching.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.060
A large focus of
his life and work

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.960
has been observing cities
through a unique modality--

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:29.950
walking.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.345
GARNETTE CADOGAN: It's
such a human pace.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.050
It allows us to inscribe
ourselves onto the world

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.810
and allows the world to inscribe
itself onto our imagination.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.290
But, more than
anything else, it's

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.320
a marvelously joyful
way of getting

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.710
to know the world, of
getting to know it up close,

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.200
not merely getting to
know it but getting

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.030
to know ourselves through
the surprising encounters we

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.450
might have.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.500
So one of the things I
love most about walking

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.540
is the way in which it
opens us up to serendipity.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.400
So walking as a way
of knowing the world,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.120
as a way of knowing
ourselves, as a way of being

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.790
open to the surprise and
serendipities of the world.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.000
SARAH HANSEN: Garnette
grew up in Jamaica,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.750
where he learned to navigate
public spaces on foot.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.150
GARNETTE CADOGAN: In
Jamaica, as I walked,

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.290
I walked a lot at night
after public transportation

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:38.050
had stopped running and would
navigate from friends' homes

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:43.240
to my home and pass through some
very dangerous neighborhoods

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:49.690
and learned how to be streetwise
as in the wisdom gained

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.740
from constant observation
and being able to anticipate

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.195
dangers or sometimes
encountering true dangers

00:02:58.195 --> 00:03:00.070
and knowing how to get
away from the dangers.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.528
And I thought that's
all you needed

00:03:01.528 --> 00:03:05.410
to do to be navigating in
public space and to be safe,

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:08.330
to anticipate the dangers
and veer away from them.

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.550
SARAH HANSEN: When he
arrived in the United States

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.730
as a college student, his
experiences with racism

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.160
began to shape the way he
traversed city streets.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.710
GARNETTE CADOGAN: And I came
to the US with that sensibility

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:27.720
and then began encountering
racism but recognized that what

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.090
actually happened then,
it wasn't so much learning

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.070
how to read the
danger but learning

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.847
to read the places in which
people thought I was a danger.

00:03:35.847 --> 00:03:38.430
That's what had happened that--
and it was over and over again

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.020
me seeing the dangers
and veering away from it

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.360
but then suddenly
recognizing and being shocked

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.480
to know that I was the danger.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.460
And I was a danger
because I was Black,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.290
because of my skin color.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.630
So navigating then
suddenly became

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.010
trying to anticipate the ways
in which people would anticipate

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.750
me being dangerous.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:02.610
It meant calibrating my
movements not to fear

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.760
but calibrating my movements
to people's fear of me

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.040
and knowing that that
could be dangerous to me,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.830
that it could mean anything
from them attacking me, which

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.110
had happened, or
threatening to attack

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:16.829
me or them calling the
police, who would then

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:20.130
give me a tough time.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:24.210
I'm able to many times
deal with awfulness

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.840
that the world throws at
me because of the beauty

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.330
that the world also presents,
that the world is never

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.320
just one thing.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.695
And so, yes, there
are people who

00:04:32.695 --> 00:04:35.640
will shout awful racist
things at me in the street.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.710
But there's also that
stranger who says, oh, I'm

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.040
going to a barbecue.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.730
And it looks like you're
just wandering and looking

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.840
for something to do.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.440
You want to come along with us?

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.390
And so that's the
very same world.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.390
SARAH HANSEN: The
very same world.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.660
There was something so
powerful in that idea.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.930
And I began to wonder how
this outlook shaped Garnette's

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.180
teaching in the classroom,
how he approached

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.370
the ideas of race and
racism with students

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.830
with this outlook in mind.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:08.030
GARNETTE CADOGAN: I focus very
much in the classroom on joy,

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:13.310
in the way in which joy becomes,
in so many ways, both a hope

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.780
and the way joy's wed to hope.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.500
But joy's also
wed to resistance.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.500
In the way joy is
wed to our ability

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.140
to deal with these things and
to push past these things.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.760
So, as a teacher,
I, time and again,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.210
try to invite students
to read the way I walk.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:37.050
It becomes a way of having a
conversation, a conversation

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.450
with the past, a conversation
with the present,

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.890
a conversation
with people nearby,

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.110
a conversation with
people who are far away,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.710
and, more than anything else,
a conversation with yourself,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.120
a way of wrestling
with yourself.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:53.940
In the end, that's my hope,
a different self can emerge.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.340
A different, better
self, much more open

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.412
to discovery and encounter.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.470
SARAH HANSEN: For Garnette,
encountering others

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:11.050
is key to understanding
the story of racism

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:14.950
in American cities and for
imagining a different, much

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:17.500
more dignified way to coexist.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:19.990
It's an idea that
grounds how he teaches

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.388
about these complexities.

00:06:22.388 --> 00:06:24.930
GARNETTE CADOGAN: What does it
mean to think of the classroom

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:29.850
as a place of encounter in which
you're continually encountering

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:34.380
these issues, these
histories, these landscapes,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.970
these complexities,
these people?

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:41.528
And what does it mean to truly
listen, to truly understand?

00:06:44.888 --> 00:06:46.430
SARAH HANSEN: Garnette
shared with me

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:49.280
that it can feel disheartening
to unpack the complexities

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.770
of racism in our society.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.890
Students and teachers alike
may experience burnout

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.840
over the course of a semester.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:59.030
As I talked with
Garnette, it struck me

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.510
that helping students cultivate
curiosity about themselves

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:07.417
and others was a strategic way
to sustain their engagement.

00:07:07.417 --> 00:07:09.500
GARNETTE CADOGAN: When you
have something so huge,

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:11.540
it's to say, oh, we're
just going to climb

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.190
this mountain right away.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:14.510
It's just impossible.

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:16.880
That your legs will
give out quite quickly.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.680
And it will just
lead to frustration.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.730
So I invite them to
just take a small patch

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:26.167
and to keep moving patch
by patch on that mountain

00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:28.250
until, at a certain point,
they'll look up and go,

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.920
oh, we're halfway up.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:32.780
And we're actually
not even trying

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.590
to conquer the mountain anymore.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.380
That was the wrong
way to go about it.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.160
We're just now at the
place in which we're

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.890
happy to investigate
the terrain and then

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:45.710
to ask each other
what the terrain looks

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.320
like where you are so you could
learn more about the mountain.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:50.270
And we might not actually
even reach the top,

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.870
but we'll have a much better
understanding of landscape

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:58.850
and of flora and
fauna and of nature

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:03.935
and a much better sense of what
it means to hike thoughtfully.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:10.730
SARAH HANSEN: As an
essayist, Garnette

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:15.050
brings an innovative slant to
teaching about race and racism.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:18.950
For him, the act of writing
and having students write

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:23.510
is a way to do the work
of encountering others.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.250
GARNETTE CADOGAN:
Writing is sometimes

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.100
just merely a way of raising
questions rather than giving

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.150
answers, particularly on
issues connected with race

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.010
and racism in cities.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.990
That's a big order.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:39.500
It's a lifetime of
thinking and wrestling

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:42.380
with complicated
issues, racism, race,

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.690
which is produced by
racism, in American cities.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:50.600
What does it take to understand
and to wrestle with in a full--

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.920
in a scope and the complications
connected with each of these.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.290
And the writing becomes a
way sometimes just merely

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.330
to ask questions that
say, oh, we have not been

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:00.560
asking the right questions.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.720
This invites us to
ask new questions.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.510
Or this invites us
to see in new ways.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.280
This invites us to listen.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.840
And, more than
anything, it invites

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:12.050
us to encounter each
other in conversation

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.330
but also encounter
others on the page.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:16.910
It means pulling back and
asking some tough questions

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.790
about our society, about
ourselves, about what it takes

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:22.700
to move forward,
understanding each other

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:26.750
but also working to advance
a much more just society

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:31.790
and a much better way of
understanding what it means

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:34.040
to coexist, what it
means to live together

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:35.450
alongside each other.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:38.040
And the writing becomes
a way to explore that.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.220
So, as they're writing,
they're suddenly

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.820
recognizing that the whole
world is their material.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.510
And they're invited to
think just expansively

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.940
and to start recognizing the
ways in which they haven't

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.455
been seeing, the ways in which
they haven't been looking.

00:09:52.455 --> 00:09:54.080
They're beginning to
ask, what might it

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.300
mean to listen to the
people who are doing harm,

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:01.580
listen to the people who
have experienced harm,

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:05.450
and to listen more
fully and broadly?

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.240
SARAH HANSEN: As my conversation
with Garnette continued,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.590
it became clear that, for
him, listening more fully

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:14.540
means tuning into
how people navigate

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.610
the world in nuanced ways.

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:19.220
It means writing about
how they encounter harm

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:23.707
but also how they cultivate
joy and hope and community.

00:10:23.707 --> 00:10:25.290
GARNETTE CADOGAN:
There's a temptation

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.900
to write about people who
have experienced racism

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.840
as just the sum total of
what has happened to them.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:35.550
So they're merely
victims with no agency.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:41.250
And they're then invited to go
beyond that by suddenly asking,

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.290
what are the ways in
which they experience joy?

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.270
What are the ways
in which they hope?

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:47.490
What are the ways
in which they work

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:50.280
towards creating a
sense of belonging

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:51.960
for themselves and others?

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:56.310
What are the tight bonds
of community and family

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.730
and country for the people
that we're writing about?

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.070
And what are the ways
in which these are

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:06.540
broken or erupted or eroded?

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:09.750
And you're not suddenly
speaking of people

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.250
in a condescending or
paternalistic matter.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:15.330
You're speaking of people
that's fully rounded,

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.670
no longer flattened.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:24.090
But you're seeing them in rich
dimension with aspirations

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:30.827
and joys and frustrations
and faults and virtues.

00:11:30.827 --> 00:11:32.660
SARAH HANSEN: Even when
writing about people

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:35.660
as fully rounded
individuals, students

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:39.110
may encounter challenges when
learning to write about race.

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:42.350
Garnette elaborated on one of
the complexities many writers

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:43.678
face.

00:11:43.678 --> 00:11:45.220
GARNETTE CADOGAN:
One of the problems

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:48.940
with speaking about
and writing about race

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:54.270
is that too often
people are made

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.510
to feel that their
responsibilities are

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:02.010
to explain one side
to another side,

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:09.021
to explain that Black experience
to people who are not Black,

00:12:09.021 --> 00:12:13.980
or to explain what it means
to inhabit a particular body

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:18.060
and set of experiences
that are unique to you

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.190
and that make moving through
or living in the world

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:27.630
a much worse experience than
someone with a different skin

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.070
color.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:34.670
But part of the challenge is to
show the ways in which racism

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:40.740
degrades everyone, that,
in degrading someone else,

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:44.670
you can't help but
degrade yourself.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:46.290
And, in that class,
one of the things

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:49.620
I'm trying to
invite people to do,

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.080
trying to invite
students to think about,

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.060
is the responsibility's
not merely

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:58.950
to think of writing about
race or understanding race,

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:02.310
of trying to show one
set of experiences

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:05.280
to someone who's foreign
to it, but to show

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.770
the ways in which we have quite
a bit of common experiences

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:14.070
and that, many times,
what is happening

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:16.020
at the heart of
racism are people

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:19.260
not merely denying other
people's dignity and humanity,

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:22.020
but denying their very
own dignity and humanity.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.480
And so it's an
invitation to figure out

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.240
how to write about race
and racism in which we're

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:30.630
showing the ways in which
we contain each other.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.550
So I keep giving them multiple
small exercises in which,

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.550
time and time again, they
begin with themselves.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:38.010
And they begin by
saying, how do I see?

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.590
Or even to write that I
want them to just think

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:41.940
about multiple voices.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.510
What does it mean to think
of writing as not just merely

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:48.120
speaking, but as a way
of integrating your voice

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:49.620
with the voice of others?

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.450
What are other people's ways
of telling your stories?

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:57.540
And how can you tell a story
about them, your honest telling

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:00.510
of their story, but in
a way that fully honors

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.730
them telling their own story.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:06.130
And that seems like such
a huge task for people.

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:10.240
And so I begin by saying, oh,
let's play critical karaoke.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.160
SARAH HANSEN: Garnette
adapted this practice

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:14.440
from a music
conference he attended

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:16.720
where attendees got
to speak about a song

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:18.310
as the song played.

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:21.730
I sat in on one of his classes
and observed critical karaoke

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:22.780
in action.

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:25.390
It was one of the most
powerful teaching methods

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:29.410
I've seen yet for capturing
how we contain each other.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.010
GARNETTE CADOGAN: They
begin by starting to say,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:36.480
this is what the song is about.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.570
Or this is why it relates
to this particular issue.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:41.370
Or my thinking on this issue.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.650
And you're hearing them
speak about the song

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:46.140
but tying the song
to a larger issue

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.930
that they're thinking
about and then

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.100
speaking about lyrics
of the song or something

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:52.260
that the song does.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:54.360
And, at that point,
their voice fades out.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.270
And you hear the
song, the song's notes

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:58.810
or the song's lyrics coming.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:02.550
And it teaches them
in a fun way how

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.130
to begin to think
about interweaving

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.350
my voice with the
voice of others, how

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:11.310
to speak alongside others
rather than about others

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:14.020
in which you're
speaking about them

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:18.600
denies them agency and dignity.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:20.940
SARAH HANSEN: Critical
karaoke is one example

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:23.940
of how Garnette is rethinking
how we engage students

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.350
in learning about racism.

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:28.570
But there's something
even more profound

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:30.670
at work in his teaching.

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:34.150
He's fundamentally
reimagining a framework

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:37.000
for how we approach the
topic with students,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:40.900
veering off a well-trod
sequential strategy

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:45.183
and jumping into a mind-opening
matrix of connections.

00:15:45.183 --> 00:15:47.350
GARNETTE CADOGAN: I'd looked
at a lot of syllabuses.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:52.330
I went online and tried to find
every syllabus I could find

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:56.620
that was available at different
universities about race

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:59.230
and racism in
America, particularly

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:00.640
in American cities.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:06.220
And the approach tends to
have it organized by issue.

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.740
This week, we will
discuss slavery.

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:12.280
Or these weeks, we will discuss
slavery, then emancipation,

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:17.290
the Civil Rights Movement,
mass incarceration, policing,

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:22.790
education, environmental
injustice, housing

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:25.820
and development, education.

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.550
And I found what
actually happened

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:32.470
was that, when I began
discussing by way of issues,

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:36.700
then people so often
organize around issues.

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:41.210
And then they felt that they had
to resolve it at that moment.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.840
SARAH HANSEN: So he
decided to shift the focus.

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:50.410
GARNETTE CADOGAN: It became on
inequality, on freedom, on joy,

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:54.880
on pessimism and hope, on
belonging, on mythology,

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:02.050
on unrest, on violence, on
resistance, on imagination.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:05.859
It was an invitation to
say we will not figure out

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:08.890
mass incarceration
in one week, that it

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:13.470
became a way of
spreading the issues out.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:15.920
And so mass incarceration
would come up

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:18.290
when we were talking
about inequality.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:20.569
And then it would return
again when we were talking

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:21.979
about hope and pessimism.

00:17:21.979 --> 00:17:23.562
And then it would
return again when we

00:17:23.562 --> 00:17:26.359
were talking about resistance.

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:31.250
And they then had the invitation
to have spent a whole semester

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.950
to think through this thing
and then suddenly recognize

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:36.110
the ways in which we
thought we were all bound up

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.527
in each other, the issues
themselves were all bound up

00:17:38.527 --> 00:17:39.110
in each other.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:42.628
As you began to think
about issues having

00:17:42.628 --> 00:17:44.045
to do with mass
incarceration, you

00:17:44.045 --> 00:17:47.443
were also thinking about issues
having to do with police.

00:17:47.443 --> 00:17:48.860
And you were
thinking about issues

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:50.060
having to do with housing.

00:17:50.060 --> 00:17:52.490
You were thinking
about education.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:56.150
You were thinking about
investment and disinvestment.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:00.050
And suddenly, instead
of in a teleogical line,

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:01.880
which is so often how
we talk about race

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:05.670
and what I recognized with
a lot of the syllabuses,

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.820
it's moving from
unfreedom to freedom

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:09.442
with occasional hiccups.

00:18:09.442 --> 00:18:11.150
That was the wrong
way to think about it,

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:14.540
rather than a matrix in which
everything was deeply connected

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:17.050
to the other.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:18.550
SARAH HANSEN:
Garnette views the act

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:22.210
of creating his syllabus as
being very similar to writing

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:24.642
the narrative of a story.

00:18:24.642 --> 00:18:26.850
GARNETTE CADOGAN: One thing
I do with the syllabus is

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:30.240
that I make the syllabus
a story in and of itself,

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:35.580
that too often syllabuses are
schematic, that they're almost

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:38.310
disconnected rooms in a house,
or they're treated like rooms

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:40.530
in this mansion, that
there's a site where you

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:41.800
can go from one to the other.

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:45.300
And I think of the
syllabus as one big hall.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:48.030
And then in a big hall
where different gatherings,

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:50.490
you can go and then
you can come back.

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.860
So you don't leave one
room, and you never return.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:55.450
And so I treat the
syllabus like a full story.

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:57.750
And so there are
songs we will hear.

00:18:57.750 --> 00:18:59.130
And then we hear
them again later

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:00.683
on in a different context.

00:19:00.683 --> 00:19:02.100
There are poems
that we will read,

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.290
and we read them again later on.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:05.940
Or there's one
particular article

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:08.400
they'll read on the second
week, and then we read it again

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:10.917
on the seventh week,
which seems like, what?

00:19:10.917 --> 00:19:12.000
Didn't we read this again?

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.100
And they suddenly
read it with new eyes.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:17.970
And it becomes a way of
seeing it as a story.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.980
And then I think of
things as leitmotifs

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:22.140
to say we will begin
discussing this.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.837
But then we will stop here.

00:19:23.837 --> 00:19:25.170
And so they get the taste of it.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.670
And then it comes back
more full-blown later on.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:30.480
And so I think of it as a
particular narrative arc,

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:33.300
so treat the semester
itself like a narrative arc.

00:19:35.513 --> 00:19:37.180
SARAH HANSEN: For
Garnette, the syllabus

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:40.450
must be as multidimensional
as the students themselves,

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:44.290
incorporating opportunities
for joy and reflection.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:48.250
And it should also be shaped
by the students' voices.

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:51.070
GARNETTE CADOGAN: Every week,
they encounter stand-up comedy.

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:52.660
And they encounter poetry.

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:54.460
They encounter song.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:57.640
They encounter film.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:03.460
They encounter short story,
fiction, essay, reportage,

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:04.870
the academic article.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:06.940
And so there are a
variety of voices.

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:11.320
And it's an invitation
to understand the world

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:13.330
in the many ways the
world presents itself

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:15.550
to you, in knowledge.

00:20:15.550 --> 00:20:18.400
One of the fascinating things
about teaching a course

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:22.120
like this is, how
do you keep yourself

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:25.510
open to being taught
by the students?

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:29.290
And thinking about this
balancing of many voices,

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:32.800
voices from the past
or voices near and far.

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:35.240
Can you accommodate
their voices?

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.090
And so, in a class
like this, one

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:42.310
has to be very open and
ready to listen and to have

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.850
students express their own
autonomy in shaping it.

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:48.130
Because there's also great
irony in not doing that

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.620
because one of the things
at the heart of racism

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.560
is the denial of
autonomy to others,

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.830
the ways in which many
times authorities used

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:00.490
to stamp on autonomy.

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:03.010
And for me not to
listen, not to have

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:08.770
them be active agents in
shaping and reshaping the class

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:13.554
would then be a version of that,
of authority denying autonomy.

00:21:16.025 --> 00:21:17.900
SARAH HANSEN: To close,
I'd like to leave you

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:22.460
with a quote from Garnette
about joy and resilience.

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:23.990
GARNETTE CADOGAN:
One of the things

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:28.920
I had made a decision very
early is that I am not the sum

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.150
total of what happens to me.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.610
It's just something I
keep reminding students

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.835
as they study about
race and racism,

00:21:34.835 --> 00:21:36.210
that people are
more than the sum

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:38.940
total of the awful things
that have happened to them,

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:43.155
that there's a possibility
of joy, of resistance,

00:21:43.155 --> 00:21:45.780
of moving through the world, not
ignoring the awful things that

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:49.110
have happened to you but of
determining that you will not

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:52.290
let these awful things
define and constrain you.

00:21:57.858 --> 00:21:59.650
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
ready to learn more

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.560
about writing and teaching
about race and racism

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:05.590
and unpacking the story
of the American city,

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:07.960
you'll soon be able to find
Garnette Cadogan's teaching

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:11.470
materials on our MIT
OpenCourseWare website.

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:13.720
Our materials are always free.

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:15.550
If you're an
educator who remixes

00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:17.680
the resources in
your own teaching,

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:20.510
please call or write to
us to share your story.

00:22:20.510 --> 00:22:22.790
You'll help inspire others.

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:24.760
Thank you so much for listening.

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:27.010
Signing off from
Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:30.040
I'm Sarah Hansen from
MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:33.449
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:47.410
AUDIO BUMPER: Ma, park the car.

